    "q-132139": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132139",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Solve the modular equation $4x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=4$ and $n=11$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,11)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ To find the inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$, we compute the exponents of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡5\\cdot 4≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{4} & ≡9\\cdot 4≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {4}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11),$ which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $11$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 3\\cdot 4x & ≡3\\cdot 7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ (3\\cdot 4)x & ≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡1\\cdot 11+10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
