    "q-213469": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213469",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Fill in the missing values, and hence solve the modular equation $6x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$ Express $x$ as a nonnegative integer smaller than $25.$\n\n${6}^{2}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${6}^{3}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${6}^{4}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n${6}^{5}\\,\\text{mod}\\,25=$\n{{field-4}}\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-5}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=6$ and $n=25$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(6,25)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$ To find the inverse of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$, we compute the exponents of $6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2} & ≡36≡25+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{3} & ≡11\\cdot 6≡66≡2\\cdot 25+16≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{4} & ≡16\\cdot 6≡96≡3\\cdot 25+21≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ {6}^{5} & ≡21\\cdot 6≡126≡5\\cdot 25+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $21\\cdot 6≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25),$ which means that $21$ is the multiplicative inverse of $6$ (modulo $25$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $21,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 21\\cdot 6x & ≡21\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ (21\\cdot 6)x & ≡63 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡2\\cdot 25+13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\ x & ≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
