    "q-213522": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213522",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Solve the following congruence:\n\n$$\n4x+3≡34\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),\n$$\n\nexpressing your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $13.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & ≡34\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x+3-3 & ≡34-3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡31\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡2\\cdot 13+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 4x & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=4$ and $n=13$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(4,13)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To find the inverse of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$, we compute the exponents of $4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16≡1\\cdot 13+3≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{4} & ≡12\\cdot 4≡48≡3\\cdot 13+9≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{5} & ≡9\\cdot 4≡36≡2\\cdot 13+10≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {4}^{6} & ≡10\\cdot 4≡40≡3\\cdot 13+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $10\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ which means that $10$ is the multiplicative inverse of $4$ (modulo $13$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $10,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 10\\cdot 4x & ≡10\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ (10\\cdot 4)x & ≡50 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡11+3\\cdot 13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ x & ≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
