    "q-78069": {
      "dom_id": "step-q78069",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Solve the congruence $15x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=43$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,43)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$ To find the inverse of $15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm. \n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}43 & = & 15\\cdot 2 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 13\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 13 & = & 2\\cdot 6 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =43-15\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2 & =15-13 \\\\ 1 & =13-2\\cdot 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =13-2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =13-(15-13)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =(43-15\\cdot 2)\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 7-15\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n1=43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20).\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $43,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}43\\cdot 7+15\\cdot (-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot (-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot (43-20) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 15\\cdot 23 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $15\\cdot 23≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43),$ which means that $23$ is the multiplicative inverse of $15$ (modulo $43$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $23,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 23\\cdot 15x & ≡23\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ (23\\cdot 15)x & ≡92 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡92 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43) \\\\ x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,43).$",
